WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.659
Film Courage: What mistakes would you say
you’ve made early on in your career with

00:00:02.659 --> 00:00:05.580
filmmaking whether it was with equipment,
timing, whatever?

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.290
Matthew Miele, Filmmaker of Always at The
Carlyle Documentary: The biggest mistake I

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:15.999
ever made (and it was an inadvertent one),
when I first was on set as a director, I didn’t

00:00:15.999 --> 00:00:17.540
spend time with the crew.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:22.859
And the reason I didn’t was because I didn’t
want them to suspect and find out that I didn’t

00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:27.160
know much and I was inexperienced and I was
doing this like total fish-out-of-water.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.749
I had no clue except for what I learned in
school.

00:00:30.749 --> 00:00:36.280
But in school you do little videos and you’re
not really directing a full on feature.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:42.000
So when the crew would have lunch, I was always
trying to separate myself because I didn’t

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.070
want to let on that like “Oh, what have
you worked on before?”

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:49.720
Or any question like that, I had no idea how
to answer it.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.039
But they saw it as (or at least my thinking.

00:00:52.039 --> 00:00:53.940
I had no idea, I had never asked them).

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.379
But I always felt like they were a little
standoffish because they were like “Oh,

00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:03.019
he doesn’t want to eat with us” or “He
wants to spend time alone.”

00:01:03.019 --> 00:01:07.130
And I always felt like it was the wrong impression
I had made obviously.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:14.040
But again it was inadvertent because I was
trying to protect myself from their more investigative

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:20.470
questions maybe about if I had any background
in this (which I didn’t).

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:26.590
So I would always say (and I still today would
say) if you are on set and you’re directing,

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:30.490
you should spend time with your crew because
it’s a true collaboration and without all

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:36.290
those voices coming in and helping you to
immerse yourselves in the story, you can’t

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:41.220
think it’s this ‘I’m The Only Voice
In This.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.650
I’m The Only Storyteller.’

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:46.550
It’s a variety of people who are going to
help you along the way and they all have their

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:49.450
own voice and creativity they are bringing
to it.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:53.380
So that’s the mutual respect across the
board that I think is super important to make

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.530
sure that I always keep that in the back of
my mind.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:02.971
And I know on my next film I’ll always really
engage more, but it’s so easy to get in

00:02:02.971 --> 00:02:11.330
your own head and to kind of just think and
brood and try to figure our how you’re going

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:12.330
to do a scene.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.960
And you feel like you’re in it alone sometimes
because the producers are only going to you

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.650
when you are losing light and you are not
making your day.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:28.800
But I feel now that I need to be a little
more involved with how the crew is feeling

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:35.000
and have lunch with them and bring the actors
in on those conversations and everyone in

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:41.150
it together rather than I think a rookie director’s
mistake is to think you are in it alone and

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:46.570
that it’s you against the world in a sense
and you kind of feel like if this doesn’t

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:49.180
go, I'll fail.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:54.170
Which is true because it’s your name on
it and if you don’t make your day and you

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.710
go over-budget, you could get fired or what
not.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:03.650
But I do think the voices on the crew are
super important to keep engaging and involve

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:06.920
on an equal level as far as their creativity.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.810
Film Courage: That’s an interesting take
on perception.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:16.200
At what point did it click for you that this
could have been one of the issues and then

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.200
you self-corrected?

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:18.960
Matthew Miele: I never cured it.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:19.960
[Laughs]

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:20.960
Film Courage: Okay.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:21.960
[Laughs]

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:26.190
Matthew Miele: I just that way on that first
set.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.230
And I tried to help it on the second set.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:35.510
But I’m a victim of my own thoughts when
I get really intense about how I going to

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:36.880
structure a scene.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:41.790
Because I don’t ever plan much, I always
try to make decisions on the day of and I

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:45.590
always try to react to what I’m given because
things change all the time.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:48.940
If you plan too much you’re in for a rude
awakening because you get to a location and

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:50.820
things always change.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:52.760
So I always try to wait until I’m there.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:04:00.630
And then when I’m there I make decisions
off-the-cuff or a little more in a way that’s

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:05.440
keeping it fresh and then I involve the actors
in those decisions and the DP, etc.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:11.650
But I would probably try to be a little more
democratic in how I am reacting to things

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:17.090
and my approach because I don’t have a shot
list generally and I don’t have a lot of

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:20.280
dialogue on how I’m doing certain things.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:23.510
So I want to make sure I communicate a little
better.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:26.630
But as I said, it’s easy to get into your
own head with these things.

